---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { meta } from '../lib/data';
import { monthLabel } from '../lib/format';

const m = meta();
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const gaps = m.unobserved_months;
---

<Base
  title="Method, and what this cannot tell you — AL Pulse"
  active="areas"
  description="How the AL Pulse figures are produced, and the things they cannot answer."
>
  <h1>Method, and what this cannot tell you</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The useful question about a dashboard is not whether it has errors but
    whether anyone is looking for them. These are the ones found so far.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
  <p>
    Every figure derives from the Registo Nacional de Turismo's public register
    of Alojamento Local. The register is scraped month by month back to 2007,
    transformed with dbt into DuckDB, and rendered here at build time. Nothing
    on this site queries anything at read time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two facts are recorded per registration: when it was registered, and whether
    it was still on the register the last time we looked. Everything else is
    derived from those.
  </p>

  <h2>The register has been pulled {m.observed_months.length} times</h2>
  <p>
    This is the single most important caveat, and it shapes every chart of
    losses. A registration that has gone can only be detected by comparing two
    consecutive pulls — so the first month any loss is visible at all is
    <strong>{monthLabel(m.loss_observable_from)}</strong>, one month after the
    first pull. Before that the loss series is not zero; it is unknown, and the
    charts leave it blank rather than draw a flat line implying that nothing
    ever lapsed.
  </p>
  {
    gaps.length > 0 && (
      <p>
        There is also a gap. The register was not pulled between{' '}
        <strong>{monthLabel(gaps[0]!)}</strong> and{' '}
        <strong>{monthLabel(gaps[gaps.length - 1]!)}</strong> — {gaps.length} months.
        Every licence that lapsed in that window is attributed to{' '}
        {monthLabel(m.data_through)}, because that is when it was first seen to
        be missing. The decline was gradual; the chart shows it as a cliff. Read
        the step as "this is what we found when we next looked", not as an event
        in that month.
      </p>
    )
  }
  <p class="small faint">
    Pulls so far: {m.observed_months.map(monthLabel).join(' · ')}.
  </p>

  <h2>What a "total" counts</h2>
  <p>
    The running total is registrations dated on or before a given month that are
    still on the register, less those seen to have gone. Its shape is reliable;
    its level is exact only at a pull month. Where this site names a peak, it
    takes the peak over pull months only — a maximum inside an unobserved
    stretch would be an artefact of when we looked.
  </p>

  <h2>What is excluded</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The Azores.</strong> Their register is not maintained to the same
      cadence, so all {m.counts.areas.toLocaleString('en-GB')} areas here are
      mainland Portugal and Madeira. An Azorean locality has no page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Areas the geography cannot place.</strong> A registration whose
      postcode resolves to no known locality is counted nationally but appears
      in no area. The postcode reference data is a fixed snapshot, so postcodes
      issued since then do not resolve.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Personal data the source withdrew</h2>
  <p>
    Until 2026-09 the public export carried the operator's name, tax number and
    contact details. It no longer does — ten columns went. Nothing on this site
    ever used them, and historical values are not shown.
  </p>

  <h2>Known open issues</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Roughly 800 registrations sit on postcodes absent from the reference data
      and so are not placed on the map.
    </li>
    <li>
      Losses in an unobserved month are attributed to the next pull, as above.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reading the charts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every mark answers at the pointer — hover, tap or focus and use the arrow keys.</li>
    <li>A change smaller than half a percentage point is rendered "no change" rather than a direction.</li>
    <li>Bars start at zero; where an axis is truncated, it is labelled.</li>
    <li>Colour never carries meaning alone — every series is also named.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Data through {monthLabel(m.data_through)}; site built {m.generated}. The
    pipeline and this site are open source.
    <a href={`${base}/`}>Back to the overview</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
